When converting a string to double using `std::stod`, if the string represents a value outside the range representable by a `double`, `std::stod` will throw a `std::out_of_range` exception. If the string does not represent a valid number, it will throw a `std::invalid_argument` exception.

Here’s how you can check whether the number in the string is out of the range of a double:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
    std::string str = /* your input */;
    try {
        double value = std::stod(str);
        std::cout << "Converted value: " << value << std::endl;
    } catch (const std::out_of_range& e) {
        std::cout << "Out of range for double: " << e.what() << std::endl;
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument& e) {
        std::cout << "Invalid argument: " << e.what() << std::endl;
    }
}
```

Alternatively, you can use `std::strtod` (from C) which sets `errno = ERANGE` if the value lies outside the range of representable values for double, without throwing exceptions:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <cerrno>
#include <cstdlib>

int main() {
    std::string str = /* your input */;
    char* endptr = nullptr;
    errno = 0;
    double value = std::strtod(str.c_str(), &endptr);
    if (errno == ERANGE) {
        std::cout << "Out of range for double" << std::endl;
    } else if (endptr == str.c_str() || *endptr != '\0') {
        std::cout << "Invalid input" << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "Converted value: " << value << std::endl;
    }
}
```

If you only want to *detect* the out-of-range case, catching `std::out_of_range` (for `std::stod`) or checking `errno == ERANGE` (for `strtod`) is the way to go. For most C++ code, using `std::stod` with exception handling is the simplest and safest way.

