A production-ready ExpandableText component in React Native should:  
- Render the text truncated to one line by default.
- Append a clickable "See more" at the end if the text overflows past one line.
- On click, expand to show all lines (i.e., full text).
- Optionally allow customization of button texts, styles, and handle edge cases in measurement.

**Key Implementation Issues:**
- Measuring if text overflows 1 line is tricky because React Native doesn’t provide this out of the box, but using `onTextLayout` we can check the number of lines rendered.
- For the "See more" to appear inline at the end, it should be rendered as a part of the main `Text` component, not as a button below, for best UX.
- Optional: Some implementations implement a "See less" when collapsed.

Here's a solid solution:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { Text, StyleSheet, View } from 'react-native';

const ExpandableText = ({
  text,
  numberOfLines = 1,
  seeMoreText = 'See more',
  seeMoreStyle,
  textStyle,
  containerStyle,
}) => {
  const [isExpanded, setIsExpanded] = useState(false);
  const [showSeeMore, setShowSeeMore] = useState(false);

  // Checks if textLayout event reports multiple lines
  const onTextLayout = React.useCallback(
    e => {
      if (!isExpanded && e.nativeEvent.lines.length > numberOfLines) {
        setShowSeeMore(true);
      }
    },
    [isExpanded, numberOfLines]
  );

  return (
    <View style={containerStyle}>
      <Text
        style={textStyle}
        numberOfLines={isExpanded ? undefined : numberOfLines}
        onTextLayout={onTextLayout}
      >
        {text}
        {!isExpanded && showSeeMore ? (
          <Text onPress={() => setIsExpanded(true)} style={[styles.seeMore, seeMoreStyle]}>
            {' '} {seeMoreText}
          </Text>
        ) : null}
      </Text>
    </View>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  seeMore: {
    color: '#007AFF',
    fontWeight: 'bold'
  },
});

export default ExpandableText;
```

**Usage:**
