My own answer:

To automatically garbage collect nodes in a linked list in C++, you should use smart pointers, specifically `std::unique_ptr` for singly-linked lists without cycles. Smart pointers automatically manage memory and cleanup resources as objects go out of scope according to the Resource Acquisition Is Initialization (RAII) principle. Here is an example implementation:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

struct Node {
    int data;
    std::unique_ptr<Node> next;
    Node(int value) : data(value), next(nullptr) {}
};

class LinkedList {
    std::unique_ptr<Node> head;
public:
    void push_front(int value) {
        auto new_node = std::make_unique<Node>(value);
        new_node->next = std::move(head);
        head = std::move(new_node);
    }

    void print() const {
        const Node* curr = head.get();
        while (curr) {
            std::cout << curr->data << " -> ";
            curr = curr->next.get();
        }
        std::cout << "nullptr" << std::endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    LinkedList list;
    list.push_front(10);
    list.push_front(20);
    list.push_front(30);
    list.print();
    // All nodes get freed automatically when 'list' goes out of scope
    return 0;
}
```
This approach leverages `std::unique_ptr`'s automatic memory management to eliminate manual `delete` calls. If you have more complex requirements, e.g., needing circular lists or shared node ownership, `std::shared_ptr` and `std::weak_ptr` may be required, but for most simple linked lists, `std::unique_ptr` is preferred for performance and safety.

