### My Answer

The prompt is to make the given Trie implementation more memory efficient. The main inefficiency is that every `Node` contains a `vector<Node*>` of size 26, regardless of how many actual children the node has. In English tries, most nodes only use 1-3 of these 26 possibilities, so this approach wastes memory.

There are a few common ways to improve memory use:

#### A) Use a fixed-size array instead of vector

If we're using a fixed alphabet (26 lowercase letters), we don't need `vector`'s dynamic size or heap allocation for the vector itself. We can use `std::array<Node*, 26>`. This will save the heap allocation and metadata per vector, but each node still uses enough space for 26 pointers, whether needed or not.

#### B) Use a hash map (unordered_map) for children

Instead of 26 pointers, use a `std::unordered_map<char, Node*>` (or `int` instead of `char` for performance), storing only the actually-present children. On sparse tries this is much more memory efficient. On dense nodes (roots of tries with every possible starting letter) it can use more memory per pointer (due to hashmap overhead), but usually still better.

#### C) Use a vector of (char, Node*) pairs

If the trie is very sparse, instead of a hash map, use `std::vector<std::pair<char, Node*>>`. For a small fixed number of children this can be cache friendly, and to find a child you scan the vector. 

#### D) Additional

- Use `nullptr` instead of `NULL`.
- Each node contains a counter. This is fine.

#### Bug Notes

The original code's insert increments count on child, but not on the current node, which may or may not be what the problem wants. (Usually, count increments at the current node when a word passes through.)

#### Applying B)

I'll show code using a hash map (the most "memory-efficient" among common approaches for sparse tries):

```cpp
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>
#include <string>

struct Node {
    std::unordered_map<char, Node*> children;
    int cnt;
    Node() : cnt(0) {}
};

class Trie {
public:
    Trie() { root = new Node(); }
    void insert(const std::string& s) {
        Node* cur = root;
        for (char ch : s) {
            // increment count for this prefix
            cur->cnt++;
            if (cur->children.find(ch) == cur->children.end()) {
                cur->children[ch] = new Node();
            }
            cur = cur->children[ch];
        }
        cur->cnt++;
    }

    int queryPrefix(const std::string& s) {
        int sum = 0;
        Node* cur = root;
        for (char ch : s) {
            if (cur->children.find(ch) == cur->children.end()) {
                break;
            }
            cur = cur->children[ch];
            sum += cur->cnt;
        }
        return sum;
    }
private:
    Node* root;
};

class Solution {
public:
    std::vector<int> sumPrefixScores(std::vector<std::string>& words) {
        Trie trie;
        for (const auto& s : words) {
            trie.insert(s);
        }
        std::vector<int> answer;
        for (const auto& s : words) {
            answer.push_back(trie.queryPrefix(s));
        }
        return answer;
    }
};
```

Alternatively, for best performance, if most nodes have children, use `std::array<Node*, 26>`. For best memory on sparse tries, use hash map. (Or in between, stick with array, but it's less efficient for memory.)

