**User prompt summary:**
- You have a div with the class `.locked-card`, which currently uses `filter: blur(6px);` for the whole card.
- You want to add a 🔒 icon to the card, _but_ you do **not** want the icon to be blurred. Only the card background (and possibly other content) should be blurred.

**Key technical point:**  
When you apply `filter: blur()` to an element, the blur applies to the entire element and all of its children. If you add the icon inside the same `.locked-card` element, it will also be blurred. To avoid this, you need to either structure the HTML so the icon is not inside the blurred element, or use a pseudo-element to blur only the background.

---

## My Answer

To show a sharp 🔒 locker icon on top of a blurred card, you need to separate the blurred part (the background) from the icon itself. Here are two common approaches:

---

**Option 1: Use a pseudo-element for the blurred background**

This technique lets you keep your HTML almost the same, and uses CSS to blur only the background:

```html
<div class="info-card locked-card" id="card7">
  <span class="lock-icon">🔒</span>
  <div class="title">Locked</div>
  <div class="value">99</div>
</div>
```

```css
.locked-card {
  position: relative;
  overflow: hidden;
  /* Remove filter from here! */
}

.locked-card::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
  filter: blur(6px);
  z-index: 1;
}

.locked-card > * {
  position: relative;
  z-index: 2;
}

.lock-icon {
  position: absolute;
  top: 10px; 
  right: 10px;
  font-size: 24px;
  /* color and other styling as desired */
}
```
- The `.locked-card` element gets a pseudo-element `::before` that covers it and has the blur filter.
- All real content (including your icon) gets a higher z-index so it’s not blurred.
- Place the icon where you want with `position: absolute;`.

---

**Option 2: Wrap the blurred content, keep the icon outside**

Alternatively, you can restructure so the blurred content is separate, and the icon is a sibling:

```html
<div class="locked-card-container">
  <div class="locked-card" id="card7">
    <div class="title">Locked</div>
    <div class="value">99</div>
  </div>
  <span class="lock-icon">🔒</span>
</div>
```

```css
.locked-card-container {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
}

.locked-card {
  background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
  filter: blur(6px);
  padding: 16px;
}

.lock-icon {
  position: absolute;
  top: 10px; 
  right: 10px;
  font-size: 24px;
  z-index: 1;
  /* other styling */
}
```
- `.locked-card` is blurred, but the icon isn't because it's a sibling, absolutely positioned over the card.

---

**In summary**:  
- Do **not** put the icon _inside_ the element with the blur filter.
- Use a pseudo-element for the blurred background, or position the icon as a sibling and absolutely overlay it.


