  - Correctly identifies components, finds the min in each, but then suggests repeatedly merging the two components with the smallest min via their representatives (using a priority queue). This essentially tries to build a MST between components via their min-price nodes.
  - However, for this cost definition (sum of two node prices), *the star approach using the global minimum is always optimal* (proof below). A Kruskal-style MST is not optimal here—because any chain built via "cheapest pair" has overall higher cost than always using the universal global min as one end.
