Let's first answer the user prompt: "I want to very low-level marshal C# HashSet<string> (generic class) instance into bytes. Please suggest code."

**My answer:**

First, it is important to clarify that HashSet<string> is a managed type, and its memory layout is not sequential or predictable. A direct memory "blit" of a HashSet<string> is not safe, portable, or reliable, and is not achievable with standard .NET low-level marshalling techniques like Marshal.StructureToPtr, because it contains references to managed objects (strings, internal arrays, etc.). You must instead perform a custom serialization.

If by "very low-level marshal" you mean to convert the contents of HashSet<string> into a compact binary format (not using .NET serialization libraries), you can manually serialize the set like this:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;

public static class HashSetMarshaller
{
    public static byte[] ToBytes(HashSet<string> set)
    {
        if (set == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(set));
        var encoding = Encoding.UTF8;
        var count = set.Count;
        var list = new List<byte>();

        // Write count as 4 bytes (little-endian)
        list.AddRange(BitConverter.GetBytes(count));

        foreach (var str in set)
        {
            var strBytes = encoding.GetBytes(str ?? "");
            // Write string length as 4 bytes
            list.AddRange(BitConverter.GetBytes(strBytes.Length));
            // Write string bytes
            list.AddRange(strBytes);
        }
        return list.ToArray();
    }

    public static HashSet<string> FromBytes(byte[] bytes)
    {
        if (bytes == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(bytes));
        int offset = 0;
        int count = BitConverter.ToInt32(bytes, offset); offset += 4;
        var set = new HashSet<string>();
        var encoding = Encoding.UTF8;

        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
        {
            int len = BitConverter.ToInt32(bytes, offset); offset += 4;
            string s = encoding.GetString(bytes, offset, len);
            offset += len;
            set.Add(s);
        }
        return set;
    }
}
```

**Summary:**
- This approach gives you a compact, order-independent binary representation of the string values in the set.
- Direct memory "blitting" of managed objects is unsafe and not supported for this case.
- If you need to interoperate with other languages or environments, ensure your encoding and integer endianness are compatible.

---

